// kldload APT pre-transaction snapshot hook.
//
// Role:     take a ZFS snapshot of the root dataset before apt changes
//           anything, so `kldload-rollback` has a point to go back to.
// Read by:  apt(8), every invocation. Placed at
//           /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/00-kldload-snapshot-pre.
// Missing:  apt still works; package transactions simply have no rollback
//           point, and kldload-rollback has nothing to offer for them.
//
// WHY the `||` matters, and why this file is not just the bare command:
//
// A Pre-Invoke hook that exits non-zero makes apt ABORT THE WHOLE
// TRANSACTION. That turned the snapshot safety net into the thing preventing
// installs: on fiend 2026-09-20 the name collided (two hooks, one second,
// second-resolution timestamps), zfs refused, apt aborted, ansible-core never
// installed, and a three-control-plane Kubernetes bootstrap failed 29 seconds
// in — three symptoms deep from a duplicate snapshot name.
//
// snapshot-create.sh now tolerates that specific collision, but the general
// rule still needs stating, and it is the rule the RPM side already follows:
// kldload-pkg-wrapper warns and proceeds when a snapshot fails, because a
// missing rollback point is recoverable and a machine that cannot install
// packages is not. The two families now agree; they did not before, and the
// half that failed closed is the half that cost a day.
//
// The warning still goes to stderr where the operator will see it, and
// snapshot-create.sh logs the reason to /var/log/kldload/snapshots.log. This
// is "fail loud", not "fail silent" — what it is not is "fail fatal".

APT::Update::Pre-Invoke {"/usr/local/sbin/snapshot-create.sh apt-pre || { echo 'kldload: WARNING — pre-transaction snapshot FAILED; proceeding without a rollback point.' >&2; echo 'kldload: see /var/log/kldload/snapshots.log' >&2; }";};
DPkg::Pre-Invoke {"/usr/local/sbin/snapshot-create.sh apt-pre || { echo 'kldload: WARNING — pre-transaction snapshot FAILED; proceeding without a rollback point.' >&2; echo 'kldload: see /var/log/kldload/snapshots.log' >&2; }";};
